1
00:00:00,170 --> 00:00:03,360
In this video, I'll be talking
about users and groups

2
00:00:03,360 --> 00:00:05,760
and how users and groups
are used in Linux.

3
00:00:05,760 --> 00:00:09,090
We'll want to understand
Linux users and groups and

4
00:00:09,090 --> 00:00:10,800
understand users and groups and

5
00:00:10,800 --> 00:00:13,720
how they interact with
Linux permissions.

6
00:00:13,880 --> 00:00:18,185
When you created your
initial Linux system image,

7
00:00:18,185 --> 00:00:20,750
you gave it a username
and a password.

8
00:00:20,750 --> 00:00:22,760
When you're accessing
that system,

9
00:00:22,760 --> 00:00:24,110
you're logging in
with that username

10
00:00:24,110 --> 00:00:25,250
and password and that's

11
00:00:25,250 --> 00:00:27,560
the authentication
that can be used

12
00:00:27,560 --> 00:00:30,185
to map a specific user account.

13
00:00:30,185 --> 00:00:32,720
Every user account on
the system is going to

14
00:00:32,720 --> 00:00:35,760
be associated with a
specific user ID or UID.

15
00:00:35,760 --> 00:00:38,060
This is just a number that

16
00:00:38,060 --> 00:00:41,365
corresponds to that particular
user on the system.

17
00:00:41,365 --> 00:00:43,760
Every process that is

18
00:00:43,760 --> 00:00:45,800
started is associated
with the UID of

19
00:00:45,800 --> 00:00:47,840
the user that either started or

20
00:00:47,840 --> 00:00:50,525
is now the owner
of that process.

21
00:00:50,525 --> 00:00:55,670
The etc/passwd, etc/password
file is what's used to

22
00:00:55,670 --> 00:00:58,400
map username to UIDs that

23
00:00:58,400 --> 00:01:02,080
are used in a given
Linux system.

24
00:01:02,080 --> 00:01:05,900
UID 0 is a special
UID that always is

25
00:01:05,900 --> 00:01:07,220
the root user and

26
00:01:07,220 --> 00:01:09,500
the root user can do almost
anything on the system.

27
00:01:09,500 --> 00:01:11,960
We talked about
Ubuntu's philosophy

28
00:01:11,960 --> 00:01:14,405
of how it uses root with pseudo.

29
00:01:14,405 --> 00:01:16,190
Once you authenticate as pseudo,

30
00:01:16,190 --> 00:01:17,300
you're essentially running as

31
00:01:17,300 --> 00:01:18,905
the root user and you can do

32
00:01:18,905 --> 00:01:22,085
pretty much anything that

33
00:01:22,085 --> 00:01:26,795
you're allowed to do via
userspace permissions.

34
00:01:26,795 --> 00:01:29,300
Every user belongs to
one or more group,

35
00:01:29,300 --> 00:01:32,840
and every group has a
corresponding group ID or GID.

36
00:01:32,840 --> 00:01:35,510
So etc group similar to

37
00:01:35,510 --> 00:01:39,065
etc password is going to
map group names to GIDs.

38
00:01:39,065 --> 00:01:43,480
We already mentioned Linux
file permissions in Week 1.

39
00:01:43,480 --> 00:01:46,480
But the permission structure
is such that you can

40
00:01:46,480 --> 00:01:49,630
associate a file with the
owning user and owning group.

41
00:01:49,630 --> 00:01:53,525
We've mentioned the UID
and GID and in high notes.

42
00:01:53,525 --> 00:01:55,450
In addition to associate

43
00:01:55,450 --> 00:01:57,220
the owning user
and owning group,

44
00:01:57,220 --> 00:02:00,400
you can also set permissions
based on the user,

45
00:02:00,400 --> 00:02:02,710
that's the owner of
the file and the group

46
00:02:02,710 --> 00:02:05,605
access for the file for
the group ownership,

47
00:02:05,605 --> 00:02:07,960
as well as the
access for everyone.

48
00:02:07,960 --> 00:02:11,110
That is done through
specific bits for read,

49
00:02:11,110 --> 00:02:13,390
write, and execute for
each of these groups.

50
00:02:13,390 --> 00:02:17,380
You can mask these bits
with given octal values.

51
00:02:17,380 --> 00:02:19,900
So adding 4 and 2 together or

52
00:02:19,900 --> 00:02:22,980
600 octal value would add

53
00:02:22,980 --> 00:02:27,430
in read and write for
the user or owner.